From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CogWasm <cogwasm@example.invalid>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: serve many compile requests from one rustc guest

wasm32-wasip1 has no threads, so a host starts a fresh rustc.wasm for every
compile. That pays guest start-up, every sysroot metadata read and fresh
linear memory each time. This adds COGWASM_SERVE=1: after the usual one-time
setup main reads NUL-separated argv requests from cogwasm.next_request, runs
compiler_entrypoint for each, reports the exit code through cogwasm.finish,
and returns when the host closes the channel. Without COGWASM_SERVE=1 main is
the one-shot path unchanged.

next_request(buf, cap) returns the request length, -n when cap is too small
(the guest retries with a larger buffer), or 0 when serving is done. On
non-wasm targets the imports are stubs that exit with an error, so the host
build still type-checks the same loop.

The second half is a process-global metadata cache in rustc_metadata, also
enabled only by COGWASM_SERVE=1. Both whole-file metadata loaders (the rmeta
path in locator::get_metadata_section and codegen's load_metadata_with) ask
it for an OwnedSlice keyed by path, length and mtime; only .rmeta and .rlib
files are cached, and any metadata error falls back to the existing loader
and its error messages.

Per-process state: the logger, signal handler, ctrlc handler and ICE hook
are installed once before the loop. ICE_PATH and ICE_COLOR keep the first
request's configuration (they are OnceLocks), and the ICE hook reports only
the first panic (FIRST_PANIC). USING_INTERNAL_FEATURES is reset by each
entrypoint. The request loop does not use std::env::args, so no per-request
argv global is involved.

Each compiler session on wasm32 runs on the calling thread (patch 0001), so
the worker-local `Registry` that a freshly spawned thread would start without
stayed attached and the second request panicked ("Thread already has a
registry"). `Registry`'s thread-local is now a `RefCell<Option<_>>` with
`Registry::unregister_current_thread`, and the wasm path of
`run_in_thread_with_globals` detaches the thread when a session ends,
including when a fatal error unwinds.
